{
  "name": "New Zealand",
  "revision": "2026-11",
  "source": "NZART New Zealand Amateur Radio Band Plans, June 2026; NZ Amateur Radio HF usage guide, February 2026. nzart.org.nz/info/band-plans. 2 m, 70 cm and 23 cm: NZART New Zealand Amateur Radio Band Plans (Bandplan-2026-June.pdf, charts and lists for 2 m, 70 cm and 23 cm, pages marked Copyright NZART April 2026), nzart.org.nz. Receiver bands (not amateur): summarised from the ITU Radio Regulations Article 5 table of allocations and general knowledge of national use. Broad, and not specific to a licence.",
  "note": "60 m is limited to 15 W EIRP, for authorised amateurs. 7.2-7.3 MHz and 51-54 MHz are secondary. 30 m shows the official plan: the NZ trial of USB voice on 10.12-10.13 MHz was set for 12 months from 1 November 2025, so check its status. On VHF and UHF: 146-148 MHz and all of 70 and 23 cm are secondary: accept interference and cause none. LIPDs use 433.05-434.79 MHz, and 1260-1270 MHz satellites are uplink only. Australian beacons on 144.4-144.6 and 432.4-432.6 MHz may be heard across the Tasman, so avoid QRM there. Digital voice simplex is 144.6-144.7 and 432.6, 432.725-432.8 MHz, with D-STAR 1296.7 MHz; hotspots are 147.4-147.45 and 438.325-438.375 MHz. The plan names no DV calling channel. 2 m repeater inputs are -600 kHz up to and including 147.000 MHz and +600 kHz above it. On 70 cm the offset is normally -5 MHz, and pairs may be reversed or use 7 MHz to avoid SRD and LIPD interference. The non-amateur bands are only offered on a receiver, and a guide to what's where, not a statement of who may use it.",
